Falsifies `system_tests::user::compositor_tests::a_client_holds_no_capability_for_its_neighbours_pixels_or_the_screen`.

Architecture: aarch64

The defect is two extra pages in the client's spawn map: the loop that maps a client's own
surface frames runs two frames past the end, so the page the attacker was handed the address
of, its neighbour's first pixel frame, is now in its own address space. Nothing else changes.
The same binary runs with the same role bits, reaches for the same virtual address, and the
address still points where the test asserts it points.

`MAX_CLIENT_MAPS` is 5 and the attacker's window needs 2 surface frames plus its control page,
so the extended loop fits exactly with no capacity edit, which is why this is one line.

Expected to fail: `assert!(wait_for(|| USER_FAULTS.load(Ordering::Relaxed) > faults), "a client
wrote at {probe_va:#x}, its neighbour's pixels, and was NOT stopped")`.

**The two preconditions above it stay green, which is what gives the red its meaning.** The
test first requires that `neighbour_probe_phys(ATTACKER)` really is the victim's first pixel
frame, so the probe cannot degenerate into poking an empty hole, and then that the attacker
reported `WIN_PROBING` at exactly the address it was handed. Both still hold under the patch:
the allocation is unchanged and the address is unchanged. Only the mapping moved.

**The confidentiality half of this test is not falsified by this patch and says so.** The test
proves four things and this reaches one: the input-slot refusal (`NoSuchSlot` on an ungranted
slot), the witness digests, and the third client's read of the screen are all downstream of
the fault assertion and never run. A record that claimed this patch falsified the whole row
would be claiming more than one assertion's worth.

